/* ============================================================================
centroid-of-triangle.epher -- centroid from the three vertices

The centroid of a triangle is the average of its vertices,
((x1 + x2 + x3) / 3, (y1 + y2 + y3) / 3) (standard formula; it is the
point where the three medians meet, two thirds of the way down each).
Points are two-number lists and the centroid prints as one. The first
example is the (0, 0), (6, 0), (3, 6) triangle, whose centroid is
(3, 2); both results are exact.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/geometry/coordinates/centroid-of-triangle.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\geometry\coordinates\centroid-of-triangle.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/geometry/coordinates/centroid-of-triangle.epher
In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

The vertices to try are in the results section below; edit them
there.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the model ----------------------------------------------------------------
def cent(p, q, r) = {(p[1] + q[1] + r[1]) / 3, (p[2] + q[2] + r[2]) / 3}

// ---- results ------------------------------------------------------------------
print("centroid of (0, 0) (6, 0) (3, 6):", cent({0, 0}, {6, 0}, {3, 6}))
print("centroid of (1, 2) (4, 6) (7, 1):", cent({1, 2}, {4, 6}, {7, 1}))
/* ---- expected output ----
= centroid of (0, 0) (6, 0) (3, 6): {3, 2}
= centroid of (1, 2) (4, 6) (7, 1): {4, 3}
*/
